## 11. Real Salesforce Example

Suppose ek company Salesforce mein **Order** create karti hai.

Order create hote hi hume external ERP system ko order details bhejni hain.

Agar hum directly trigger se API call karein:

```text
Order Created
     ↓
Trigger
     ↓
API Call
     ↓
Response
     ↓
Transaction Complete
```

Isse main transaction external API par dependent ho sakti hai.

Future Method approach:

```text
Order Created
     ↓
Trigger
     ↓
Future Method Called
     ↓
Main Transaction Complete
     
Meanwhile...

Future Queue
     ↓
Future Method Executes
     ↓
ERP API Call
```

**But:** new development ke liye Salesforce documentation Queueable Apex ko prefer karne ko kehti hai, so practical modern design mein Queueable ko evaluate karna chahiye. 

---

## 12. Interview mein 30-second answer

> **"Future Method Salesforce Apex ka asynchronous mechanism hai. Iska use hum aise operations ke liye karte hain jo background mein execute ho sakte hain, jaise external API call ya kuch DML operations ko separate transaction mein execute karna. Future Method `@future` annotation ke saath define hota hai, static aur void hona chahiye, aur isme sObjects directly pass nahi kar sakte—usually unki IDs pass karke latest records query karte hain. Callout ke liye `@future(callout=true)` use hota hai. Testing ke liye `Test.startTest()` aur `Test.stopTest()` use karte hain. Salesforce new development ke liye Queueable Apex ko Future Methods ke upar recommend karta hai."**  

Agar aap Salesforce **interview preparation** kar rahe ho, toh is topic ko yaad rakhne ka easiest formula hai:

**Future = Async + `@future` + static void + no sObject parameter + callout possible + separate execution + Queueable preferred.**
